  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Prolonged period of dry weather continues for \nthe remainder of the week along with a large diurnal temperature\nrange.\n\nUpper level ridging and surface high pressure continue to govern the \noverall pattern through the rest of the week. This will keep \nconditions dry overall with a large diurnal temperature range. 925 \nmb temperatures generally sit at 10-12C through the week. This \ncombined with continued overall W flow will keep the region warmer \nthan normal during the day with highs into the low and even mid 60s. \nMostly clear skies with light to calm winds expected for tonight as \nwell, which will be supportive of strong radiational cooling that \nwill drop lows in the traditional cooler spots into 20s. Elsewhere, \nlows will generally fall to the 30s with the coastal areas slightly \nwarmer. We could see a similar setup once again Thursday night, \nthough there's a chance for some mid to high level clouds moving in \nearly Friday morning that could limit the extent of radiational \ncooling.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Monitoring a possible coastal storm which could\nbring a windswept heavy rain, marine impacts and possible tidal\nflooding late this weekend into early next week but the details\nare still uncertain. \n\nWe are still monitoring for the possible development of a coastal \nlow pressure near the Carolinas/Florida Gulf Coast vicinity late in \nthe week and possibly impact Southern New England either late this \nweekend or early next week. This feature, tied to an upper level \ntrough over the east-central Gulf Coast is associated with active \nthunderstorms over the eastern Florida coast, and is forecast to \nmove northward and then recurve. There are a number of uncertainties \nregarding this feature; the system's track, how much/how quickly it \ndevelops, and when/where the recurve out to sea is still unclear at \nthis juncture. Ensemble membership analysis show a wide range of \npotential track locations from an inland runner track through the \nmid-Atlantic to something well offshore/well south of Nantucket and \nin between. \n\nSome impacts for Southern New England could be possible but too \nearly to know what those could look like given the large uncertainty \nregarding the track and strength. Besides this system being the next \nbest chance at rain, marine impacts in the form of increasingly \ngusty marine winds as well as building rough seas could be among \nthose. It's also worth noting that astro tides begin to rise this \nweekend into Monday; by Monday morning the astro tide at Boston \nHarbor is predicted to be around 11.1 ft MLLW, so only a 1-1.5 ft \nstorm surge would be enough to cause coastal flooding problems. At \nthis point will continue to monitor developments in the\nguidance.",
